{"name":"Venus Deva","hex":"#8f7974","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"venus","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the bivalve molluscs in the genus Venus or family Veneridae."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/venus"},{"term":"deva","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A god in Vedic mythology, Hinduism and Buddhism."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/deva"}],"entries":[{"title":"Deva (Hinduism)","blocks":[{"text":"Deva means 'god', 'shiny', 'exalted', 'heavenly being', 'divine being', 'anything of excellence', and is also one of the Sanskrit terms used to indicate a deity in Hinduism. Deva is a masculine term; the feminine equivalent is Devi. The word is a cognate with Latin deus ('god') and Greek Zeus."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Indra%2C_Chief_of_the_Gods_LACMA_M.69.13.4_%281_of_5%29.jpg/330px-Indra%2C_Chief_of_the_Gods_LACMA_M.69.13.4_%281_of_5%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deva_(Hinduism)","wikidata":"Q211258"},{"title":"Venus","blocks":[{"text":"Venus is the second planet from the Sun. Similar in size and mass to Earth, Venus has no liquid water, and its atmosphere is far thicker and denser than that of any other rocky body in the Solar System. The atmosphere is composed mostly of carbon dioxide and has a thick cloud layer of sulfuric acid that spans the whole planet. At the mean surface level, the atmosphere reaches a temperature of 737 K, making it the hottest planet in the solar system and also a pressure 92 times greater than Earth's at sea level, turning the lowest layer of the atmosphere into a supercritical fluid. From Earth, Venus is visible as a star-like point of light, appearing brighter than any other natural point of light in the sky, as either the brightest \"morning star\" or \"evening star\". It was also given the poetic name Ēarendel in Old English, meaning \"ray of light\" along with the two previous epithets."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Venus_from_Mariner_10.jpg/330px-Venus_from_Mariner_10.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venus","wikidata":"Q313"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/A_havan_ceremony_on_the_banks_of_Ganges%2C_Muni_ki_Reti%2C_Rishikesh.jpg/500px-A_havan_ceremony_on_the_banks_of_Ganges%2C_Muni_ki_Reti%2C_Rishikesh.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/A_havan_ceremony_on_the_banks_of_Ganges%2C_Muni_ki_Reti%2C_Rishikesh.jpg/500px-A_havan_ceremony_on_the_banks_of_Ganges%2C_Muni_ki_Reti%2C_Rishikesh.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Shiva/Rudra has been a major Deva in Hinduism since the Vedic times.[24] Above is a meditating statue of him in the Himalayas with Hindus offering prayers.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_havan_ceremony_on_the_banks_of_Ganges%2C_Muni_ki_Reti%2C_Rishikesh.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Tani_Bunch%C5%8D_-_Jy%C5%ABroku_Zenshin_no_Zu.jpg/330px-Tani_Bunch%C5%8D_-_Jy%C5%ABroku_Zenshin_no_Zu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Tani_Bunch%C5%8D_-_Jy%C5%ABroku_Zenshin_no_Zu.jpg/330px-Tani_Bunch%C5%8D_-_Jy%C5%ABroku_Zenshin_no_Zu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The 12 Devas protecting Buddha, by Tani Bunchō. The Hindu Devas were adopted by Japanese Buddhists in the first millennium as Jūni-ten[25]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATani_Bunch%C5%8D_-_Jy%C5%ABroku_Zenshin_no_Zu.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thum